-- title:
--   Finite flat algebras over a local ring are finitely presented
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local ring and let $C$ be a commutative ring equipped with an $R$-algebra structure, such that $C$ is finite as an $R$-module (generated by finitely many elements) and flat as an $R$-module. The conclusion is that $C$ is a finitely presented $R$-algebra: there are finitely many elements $c_1,\dots,c_n$ of $C$ generating it as an $R$-algebra, so that $C$ is a quotient of the polynomial ring $R[X_1,\dots,X_n]$, and the kernel of the corresponding surjection is a finitely generated ideal. Note that the hypothesis is finiteness and flatness as a module, not merely as an algebra, and that locality of $R$ is used (no Noetherian or reducedness assumption is imposed); the assertion is about presentability as an algebra, which is the form in which it is needed, and it is deduced from the stronger statement that $C$ is finitely presented as an $R$-module.
--
--   This is the standard fact that over a local base a module-finite flat algebra is of finite presentation, the affine-local ingredient behind the equivalence of 'finite flat' and 'finite locally free'. It is used in the construction of finite faithfully flat base changes in the study of abelian schemes with good reduction, where finite presentation of the algebra is required to invoke the corresponding scheme-theoretic statements.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Algebra_finitePresentation_of_finite_of_flat_of_isLocalRing.lean

